   "text": "Here is how I read the structure and built the SMILES.\n\n**Pyrazine fragment (left).** The ring runs C(OMe), N, C(OH), C(carboxamide), N, C(Cl). The carboxamide C(=O)NH leaves the ring carbon that sits between the C(OH) and the second N.\n\n**Nine-membered dilactone ring.** Going around the ring from the NH-bearing carbon:\n- CH(NH-acyl)\n- C1(=O)\n- O\n- CH(CH3)\n- CH(O-isobutyryl)\n- CH(CH2Ph)\n- C(=O)\n- O\n- CH2, which closes back to the CH(NH-acyl)\n\nThe ester on the ring is isobutyrate, OC(=O)CH(CH3)2. The substituent on the ring carbon next to the lactone carbonyl is a benzyl group.\n\n**Stereochemistry.** I assigned each centre from the wedges and hashes, ranking substituents by CIP priority and taking the implicit H as opposite the wedge or hash:\n- The NH-bearing carbon has a hashed N and is S.\n- The CH(CH2Ph) carbon has a wedged benzyl and is R.\n- The CH(O-isobutyryl) carbon has a hashed O and is R.\n- The CH(CH3) carbon has a wedged methyl and is S.\n\nI then converted these to @/@@ according to the neighbour order in the SMILES string.\n\nSMILES: COc1nc(O)c(C(=O)N[C@H]2COC(=O)[C@H](Cc3ccccc3)[C@@H](OC(=O)C(C)C)[C@H](C)OC2=O)nc1Cl"
